  - [3mRules out later:[0m nothing, but you carry the script-ordering hazard indefinitely.

  [1mC. Convert the repo to ES modules, then build the subsystem natively.[0m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, [38;5;153msrc/[39m moves
  from [38;5;153mrequire[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, and [38;5;153mlogging/[39m uses plain [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m that both Node
  and browsers run natively — still no build step.
  - [3mBuys:[0m one module system across the repo. Real encapsulation with no globals, no UMD footers, no load-order coupling,
    and the module graph is explicit. The two-module-system split is the direct cause of the awkwardness in A and B, so
    fixing it is targeted at this work rather than drive-by refactoring.
  - [3mCosts:[0m it edits files the logging task wouldn't otherwise touch — [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m,
    [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. And ES modules are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m by CORS, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops
    working; you'd need to serve it ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar). If anyone's local workflow is "open the file,"
    that's a real regression.
  - [3mRules out later:[0m effectively locks the repo into ESM. Going back is another conversion.

  [1mMy recommendation: C.[0m The reason is that the split module systems aren't incidental to this task — they're the thing
  that makes A messy and B fragile, and both A and B pay for the split forever in order to avoid touching it once. On
  the [38;5;153mfile://[39m cost specifically: your remote sink needs a real origin to POST from, and production serves over HTTP
  regardless, so that limitation bites casual local browsing rather than the use case you're actually building for.

  If you'd rather this change stay strictly additive and leave existing files' style alone, [1mB[0m is the right call and I'd
  take it over A — the script-ordering hazard is annoying but visible, whereas A's environment branching quietly rots.
